## The AVS showed both adrenal glands overproducing. The surgeon’s answer: medication first, surgery only as a last resort.

> *This is My Ledger, the personal side of v64otd.com. What follows is my experience and opinion, written in the first person, with the facts sourced at the bottom. I’m not a doctor. This is my experience, not medical advice. Talk to your own doctor about your care.*

Quick recap if you’re just joining. [Chapter 1](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-1/) covered seventeen years of diagnoses that turned out to share one cause nobody connected. [Chapter 2](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-2/) covered the diagnosis itself: masses on both adrenal glands, Cushing’s confirmed, and a multidisciplinary team at UT Southwestern taking the case. [Chapter 3](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-3/) laid out the numbers, ACTH-independent with a 24-hour urine cortisol of 221 mcg, and covered a GI visit where I brought six pages of prepared questions and left with most of them unanswered. [Chapter 4](https://v64otd.com/ledger/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-4/) covered the endoscopy I asked for, was turned down for, then got anyway, and the pathology that pointed to Autoimmune Metaplastic Atrophic Gastritis, or AMAG: ECL cell hyperplasia, focal mucosal atrophy, and focal intestinal metaplasia.

This chapter is about what October delivered, and what the surgeon told me at the end of November.

### What adrenal vein sampling is, and why it matters

For anyone who hasn’t followed since Chapter 2, here’s the problem adrenal vein sampling exists to solve.

Bilateral adrenal adenomas mean masses on both glands. Cushing’s confirmed means one or both of those glands are making cortisol they shouldn’t, and have probably been doing it for seventeen years or longer if the timeline in Chapter 1 is right. But “both glands have masses” and “both glands are misbehaving” are two different things. One gland can be the main driver while the other is just along for the ride, abnormal on a scan but not the source of the problem. Or both can be contributing. Take out the wrong gland, and the problem doesn’t go away. Take out both when only one was the problem, and you’re facing adrenal insufficiency, where your body can’t make cortisol at all, and replacement medication for the rest of your life. That’s not a small risk.

Adrenal vein sampling settles the question before anyone picks up a scalpel. A catheter is placed into the vein draining each adrenal gland; they draw blood from both sides and compare the cortisol coming out of each against the baseline in the inferior vena cava, the big vein the adrenals drain into. If one side is running much higher, the numbers show it. If both sides look about the same, that tells a different story and changes what surgery should look like.

My AVS was scheduled for October 26th at UT Southwestern, and was done by a specialist in Interventional Radiology. I put together a written summary for the procedure team beforehand, same as I’ve done for every big appointment. The biggest technical worry with AVS is whether the radiologist can get the catheter into both adrenal veins. The right one is notoriously hard to reach, and it’s the most common reason a procedure comes back inconclusive.

October 26th, it was. Moderate sedation, both sides sampled, home the same day.

### What the procedure found

Under dexamethasone suppression, the right adrenal vein came back at 23.5 times the cortisol level in the inferior vena cava. One commonly used cutoff in this kind of testing calls an adrenal vein autonomous, meaning producing cortisol on its own, when it runs more than 6.5 times the baseline. The right side is way past that. That’s not a close call.

The complication is the left side. The left samples ranged from 6 to 7.3 times the baseline. That’s right around the same cutoff, and it’s not suppressed. In most cases like mine, when one side is clearly dominant, the other side is basically shut down because all that extra cortisol from the dominant gland suppresses the ACTH signal the other side depends on. My left side didn’t get that memo. Both glands are producing on their own. The right is just doing it at a much higher level, roughly three to four times the left. That’s what makes my case unusual, and it’s why there’s no straightforward path to surgery.

The AVS was the last big piece of the adrenal puzzle. Everything before itthe CT in June, the Cushing’s confirmation in August, the multidisciplinary review, the months of labs- was building to this result. Now we have it. What to do with it is the conversation that started November 17th.

### October 12th: the GI follow-up

Before the AVS, I had a GI follow-up on October 12th. My GI doctor has handled my colonoscopies for twelve years. This was supposed to be a review of the endoscopy findings from Chapter 4 and a plan for managing AMAG.

His PA confirmed the findings I’d already read in the pathology report myself. That was the visit.

I’d put together a detailed question sheet before I went in. Diet considerations for AMAG, what the surveillance schedule for the ECL hyperplasia should look like, whether serum gastrin should be established as a baseline, whether chromogranin A made sense given the carcinoid risk, B12 management, copper levels, whether injectable B12 was appropriate. None of it got answered. No monitoring plan, no symptom guidance, nothing about what to expect going forward. The only thing I left with was a recommendation to take 1,000 micrograms of sublingual B12. I was already doing that.

The antibodies were ordered at that visit and came back a few days later. Anti-parietal cell at 122.7, more than six times the upper limit of normal. Anti-intrinsic factor at 28.2, more than 25 times the cutoff. The parietal cell antibodies mean my immune system is attacking the stomach cells that make acid. Intrinsic factor antibodies mean that the protein that carries B12 across the gut wall is itself under attack. Those aren’t borderline numbers. Together with the pathology, they confirmed the AMAG diagnosis.

At the end of that week, I sent a detailed message through the patient portal laying out the unanswered questions. The gastrin, the MMA, the difference between serum B12 and how B12 is actually working at the cellular level, all of it. The office's response: schedule another appointment to discuss your concerns.

That’s when I decided I was done.

I still needed the MMA and homocysteine. I happened to be going in for my second shingles vaccine, brought it up with the PA, explained my reasoning, and she ordered them on the spot. MMA is the functional marker for B12. It rises when cells aren’t getting enough B12 to do their work, regardless of what the serum level shows. Mine came back at 229 nmol/L, right in the middle of the normal range. Not high, not creeping up. Funny thing is, that’s exactly why I’m watching it. I’ve got AMAG and intrinsic factor antibodies more than 25 times over the cutoff. If my MMA were climbing on top of that, I wouldn’t need anybody to connect the dots for me. But it’s not. It’s normal, and now I have a normal number to measure against. What it does over the next few years is what I’ll be watching. Homocysteine at 11 is normal too. Together they give me a baseline.

The rest of the outstanding labs, serum gastrin, chromogranin A, and direct copper, will have to wait until I see the GI specialist at UTSW in March 2027. That’s the referral I got after leaving that practice. My endocrine surgery team is already at UTSW. AMAG raises the risk of gastric neuroendocrine tumors and gastric adenocarcinoma down the road, and if anything ever progresses past hyperplasia, I want to already be in the right building with people who coordinate with each other.

One more October item from Chapter 4: the FibroScan on October 16th never happened. The fasting instruction was only in a text message, not in my appointment notes in the portal, so I showed up without fasting. I’m not rescheduling it there. I’ll take it up with my new GI specialist at UTSW in March, so the liver questions from Chapter 4 stay open until then.

### The lung scan I almost didn’t schedule

There’s a finding worth noting that has nothing to do with the adrenal story, but it belongs in the record.

On September 4th I had a low-dose CT chest scan for lung cancer screening. I’m a former smoker, roughly twenty pack-years, and I quit in 2024. The result was Lung-RADS 2, which means a benign appearance and a very low likelihood of cancer. Specifically: a 5 mm solid nodule in the lateral right lower lobe and a calcified granuloma in the lingula. The granuloma is benign and needs no follow-up. Annual low-dose CT from here on, with the next one around September 2027.

The incidental findings worth flagging: that same scan picked up calcific coronary artery atherosclerosis and mild diffuse bronchial wall thickening. I’m not a radiologist, and I’m not interpreting what those mean. I’m noting that a scan I scheduled to check one box came back with two things that belong on somebody else’s radar.

One thing worth knowing: I had pneumonia as a small child, so some of what showed up on that scan could be scarring from that.

The coronary calcification was already on my cardiologist’s radar. He did a cardiac CT scan on me in 2025 that showed some calcification, and he told me then it wasn’t something to be concerned about.

I saw him on November 20th. It was our regular six-month check-in. He’s been my cardiologist for a while now, and we’ve been on a six-month schedule for about a year and a half. This relationship is one I owe more than I’ve said anywhere in this series. He’s the one who first pushed me to use AI to dig into my own medical history and find the thread that connected everything. Without that conversation, I don’t write Chapter 1.

I caught him up on everything. The adrenal tumors, the Cushing’s confirmation, the AVS results, the AMAG, the GI experience, the whole run. When I told him about the GI doctor and my old endocrinologist, he didn’t defend them. He said many physicians still look at everything through the lens of which pill fixes it. He thinks AI is going to force that to change, pushing doctors to treat patients as complex, individual systems rather than as a checklist of separate problems. I didn’t argue with him.

He scheduled me for a transthoracic echocardiogram, in preparation for adrenal surgery in case it becomes necessary. One opened up the next day, so I took it on November 21st. He wasn’t waiting for surgery to get scheduled before doing his part. The echo puts his part of the cardiac clearance on record now, so if surgery ever does get a date, there’s no delay on his end. I don’t have the results yet.

He also flagged something to watch. Hypercortisolism raises blood pressure. If treatment brings my cortisol down, my blood pressure may come down on its own, and he wants to watch it closely to see whether the medication I’m on still needs to be there.

Six-month follow-up scheduled, same as always.

### The labs that have been piling up

October is also when a pile of labs I’d been waiting on finally came back.

Ferritin came back at 125. That’s normal, after sitting at 6 in May. It took five months of iron supplements to get there, with a stomach that was fighting the process the whole time. AMAG explains both why it took that long and why iron isn’t a problem I solve once and move on from. Iron is absorbed best in the proximal small intestine with help from stomach acid, and AMAG slowly removes that acid.

Vitamin D at 81 ng/mL is good. The D3/K2 routine is doing what it’s supposed to. On the lipid side, Repatha has LDL down to 87, but HDL is sitting at 36, still below 40. The working hypothesis is that HDL should improve once the cortisol comes down, because hypercortisolism suppresses it, but that’s my hypothesis for now. HbA1c at 6.4% is well controlled. The ACR, the albumin-to-creatinine ratio that measures protein leaking into the urine, came in at 188 in July, up from 125 in May. That trend is on my PCP’s radar, with Losartan already in the picture, and whether we need to do more is on the agenda for the next visit.

Hemoglobin 18.0, hematocrit 53.8, red blood cell count 6.55. That’s erythrocytosis, too many red blood cells, most likely driven by Jatenzo, the oral testosterone I take. Testosterone pushes up red blood cell production. The line in the sand is a hematocrit of 54%. Below that, you watch it. Above it, the conversation changes. I’m at 53.8. That number is going to my endocrine team and my PCP, and it’ll be part of the Jatenzo review as treatment gets going.

### The call that changed the conversation

On the morning of November 17th, my endocrinologist called me herself to walk me through what happened at the multidisciplinary conference the Friday before, when the team presented my case with the AVS results.

She said my situation is unique. No textbook case for it. The right side at 23.5 times the baseline is a clear signal. The left side also producing, not as much but enough, is what makes it complicated. In most cases like this, the answer is simple: take out the dominant gland, the suppressed side picks up the slack, and you’re done. Mine doesn’t work that way, because the left side isn’t suppressed. It’s producing on its own. Removing the right doesn’t fully fix it.

She went through the options the team talked about. On one end: remove the right gland and use medication to manage whatever the left keeps putting out. On the other end: skip surgery entirely and try to control both glands with medication. There’s also the possibility of ablation on the remaining gland if medication can’t hold the left side down the road.

She asked what I thought. I told her I’d be in favor of removing the right and trying to manage the left with medication. My thinking was simple. With the right side that far out, holding it down with medication alone would take a lot of drugs, and more drugs means more exposure and more risk of side effects. Taking the right gland out removes the biggest source of the problem cleanly. The left becomes something to manage instead of the whole battle. She said she tended to agree.

I asked the question that had been sitting in the back of my mind: what are the odds I end up needing both glands out eventually, on cortisol replacement for life? She said not necessarily. These tumors grow slowly. The left could get worse, or it could stay where it is. There’s no way to predict it, but it’s not inevitable. And if it does progress, there are options short of taking the whole gland. Ablation is one of them.

That left one appointment: the endocrine surgeon at UT Southwestern on November 24th. Fellowship-trained, the same surgeon whose team reviewed my case at the original multidisciplinary conference back in Chapter 2, and whose team presented it again on November 13th. She had the full picture.

### November 24th: the surgeon’s answer

The surgeon was compassionate, cautious and empathetic. She started by explaining what they were looking for when they sampled my adrenal veins in October. I’ll be honest: I didn’t follow all of it. The short version is that both adrenal glands are overproducing.

She said the hardest part of my case is the unknown. Nobody can say for sure what removing one gland would do when the otheris also overproducing. Medication might keep the remaining gland in check, or we might end up right back in the operating room to take out the other one. Removing one gland and then managing what the other one puts out is the toughest version of this, and she doesn’t like to take out both glands unless it’s absolutely necessary.

She explained that some people with Cushing’s do need both glands removed. They’re in bad shape, with all the classic hard physical signs, like the stretch marks and the days they can’t get out of bed. She’d rather not start there with me.

So the plan is medication first. The goal is to bring down the effects of the extra cortisol with medication and see how my body responds. That will take a lot of coordination with my medical endocrinologist to get the dosing right. They want my cortisol levels down, but not so far down that I end up with too little, which is adrenal insufficiency, the same risk that came up back at the start of this chapter. If the medication works, great. If it doesn’t, surgery is still on the table as a last resort.

She also walked me through the risks of surgery. There’s bleeding. There’s the chance of damage to the liver or other organs that have to be moved aside to reach the adrenal glands. And if both glands come out, she said, recovery can take a couple of years.

That one stopped me. I knew recovery could take a while. I had no idea it could take that long.

That’s not where I expected November to end. A week earlier, I’d told my endocrinologist I leaned toward taking the right gland out. The surgeon’s plan starts somewhere else, and she laid out why.

As we were winding down, I thanked her for being candid and for not being scalpel-happy. She chuckled and told me something about the conference on my case. The surgeons in the room were all against surgery up front. The medical endocrinologists thought it should be fine to remove one gland and treat the other. The surgeons were the ones arguing against surgery.

To me, that conference is how this is supposed to work: surgeons and endocrinologists looking at the same case, disagreeing out loud, and working it out before anyone touches me. My old endocrinologist was going to try to treat this on his own with medication. Looking back, medication first may have been the right approach all along. I still believe I made the right call insisting on a surgical consult at UTSW and getting a new endocrinologist. I’d rather have a team arguing over the options than one person rolling the dice.

Now I wait to hear from my medical endocrinologist about how we start the medication.

### The part that keeps coming back to me

Near the end of Chapter 4, I wrote that I’m not a doctor, I shouldn’t be the one holding all of this together, and I’m doing it anyway.

When I started this chapter, I thought it would end with a handoff. The workup was done, the picture was as complete as I could make it, and the decision belonged to the surgeon. I figured the next chapter would be about surgery.

It isn’t. The diagnosis is done, but the work isn’t. Medication means dosing, labs, adjustments, more labs, and a surgeon and an endocrinologist who need to stay in step with each other. It means watching numbers that can go wrong in either direction. For the past six months, I’ve been the one tracking numbers and connecting things that weren’t getting connected. That job isn’t ending. It’s changing shape again.

There’s a payoff I’m hoping for at the end of this. Excess cortisol is known to push up blood pressure, blood sugar, and cholesterol. I take medication for all three, and I believe the hypercortisolism has been driving them all along. If the cortisol comes down and stays down, I’m hoping some of those prescriptions come off the list. That’s the finish line I’m looking at.

So this series isn’t ending. From here it becomes periodic updates: how the medication is going, what’s working, what isn’t, and whatever comes up along the way.

> *None of this is medical advice, mine or yours. I’m describing what I’ve been through and what these tests and procedures are for, in plain language. What any of it means for a particular person is a conversation for that person’s own doctors.*

